   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "The three verb families",
     "texto": [
      "In Spanish, verbs are grouped into three conjugation families based on their infinitive: **-ar**, **-er**, and **-ir**.\n\n- **hablar**: hablo, hablas, habla, hablamos, habláis, hablan\n- **comer**: como, comes, come, comemos, coméis, comen\n- **vivir**: vivo, vives, vive, vivimos, vivís, viven\n\nThe forms for **yo, tú, él, and ellos** keep the stress on the stem: *HA-blo, HA-blas, HA-bla, HA-blan*. For **nosotros and vosotros**, the stress shifts to the ending: *ha-BLA-mos, ha-BLAIS*.\n\nSubject pronouns are usually dropped because the ending makes it clear who is doing the action. After the verb hablar, language names omit the article: **hablo español**, **hablamos inglés**."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Hablar, comer, and vivir",
     "texto": [
      "Notice how the endings change when talking about yourself or about a group:\n\n- **Yo**: hablo, como, vivo (all end in -o).\n- **Nosotros**: hablamos (-ar), comemos (-er), vivimos (-ir).\n\nWhen naming a language after hablar, do not use an article: say «hablo español», never «hablo el español»."
     ],
